## What Happened

`DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS because **Apple removed DSA support from its native cryptographic frameworks** (Security.framework / CommonCrypto). .NET's cryptography layer on macOS delegates to these native libraries, so when Apple dropped DSA, .NET surfaces the error at runtime.

This breaking change was introduced when .NET aligned with the platform's crypto capabilities — DSA has been unsupported on macOS since Apple deprecated it. Your .NET 10 build likely either didn't target macOS or used a runtime that still had a fallback; .NET 11 enforces the platform restriction.

## How to Fix

**Option 1 — Use BouncyCastle (recommended if you must verify legacy signatures on all platforms):**

```csharp
// Install: dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
verifier.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

**Option 2 — Conditional platform logic (if macOS verification isn't critical):**

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // verify signature
}
else
{
    // fall back or skip
}
```

**Option 3 — Migrate off DSA entirely (best long-term):**

DSA is considered legacy/weak. Re-sign with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** (supported natively on all platforms in modern .NET) and retire the DSA keys.

---

**TL;DR:** Apple killed DSA → .NET 11 on macOS can't create DSA keys. Use BouncyCastle for a portable software implementation, or migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519.
